==English==

===Noun===
{{en-noun|sg=[[run]] [[out]]}}

# {{alternative spelling of|runout}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|inf=[[run]] [[out]]|runs out|running out|ran out|run out}}

# {{idiomatic}} To [[use up]]; to [[consume]] all of something.
#: ''If this hot weather continues, we will '''run out''' of ice cream.''
# to expire, to come to an end
#* {{quote-news
|year=2011
|date=April 11
|author=Phil McNulty  
|title=Liverpool   3 - 0   Man City
|work=BBC Sport
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/9449111.stm
|page=
|passage=Mario Balotelli replaced Tevez but his contribution was so negligible that he suffered the indignity of being substituted himself as time '''ran out''', a development that encapsulated a wretched 90 minutes for City and boss Roberto Mancini. }}
# {{cricket}} To get a batsman out via a run out (''see above''); ''or'', to be got out in this way.
# Of a legal right, to [[expire]], to [[terminate]].
#: ''My driving licence '''runs out''' next week, so I had better renew it now.''
#: ''The option will '''run out''' next week and I can't get it extended.''
# To [[extend]] a piece of material, or clothing.
#: ''If I '''run out''' these curtains, they will fit the windows in the drawing room.''
# To [[conclude]] in, to [[end up]]
#* {{quote-news
|year=2011
|date=September 29
|author=Jon Smith
|title=Tottenham   3 - 1   Shamrock Rovers
|work=BBC Sport
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/15014632.stm
|page=
|passage=Tottenham survived a scare as they fought back from 1-0 down to '''run out''' comfortable winners against Shamrock Rovers in the Europa League.}}

